 "drivingQuestion": "What does the mishna mean by valuations that are movables?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "Unit ends mid-citation; continued in the next sugya",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sanhedrin.14b.14",
   "daf": "14b",
   "sentenceId": 13,
   "he": "הָעֲרָכִין כּוּ׳. מַאי עֲרָכִין הַמִּטַּלְטְלִין? אָמַר רַב גִּידֵּל אָמַר רַב: בְּאוֹמֵר ״עֵרֶךְ כְּלִי זֶה עָלַי״. דְּאָמַר רַב גִּידֵּל אָמַר רַב:",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches that «the valuations» that are movable property are performed by three judges. The Gemara asks: «What» is the meaning of: «Valuations that are movable property?» The term: Valuations, generally is referring to a unique type of calculation in which one vows to contribute the value of a person, according to the set amounts explicitly stated in the Torah (see Leviticus 27:1–8). This type of calculation does not apply to property, so to what does the mishna refer? «Rav Giddel says» that «Rav says:» The mishna was discussing a case «where one says: The valuation of this utensil» is incumbent «upon me» to contribute. «As Rav Giddel says» that «Rav says:»"
  }
 ],
